    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d852">

                <pb facs="1388" n="1372" sameAs="frus:frus1951v07p1/pg1372"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Lot 55D128: Black Book, Tab 105: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Commander in Chief, Far East</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Ridgway</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Joint Chiefs of Staff
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">priority</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1951-12-18T16:47:00+09:00">18 December
                            1951—4:47 p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>C–59618. For info CINCUNC Adv 585.</p>
                <p>“Meeting opened 1100 on item 3. Hsieh referred to UNC insistence on rotation and replacement, and to his side’s
                    objection to ‘interference in internal affairs.’ UNC repeated position of rotation and replacement and airfields
                    from record of Dec 15. Hsieh stated the 2 questions above must be settled.
                        UNC asked if Hsieh had anything new to
                    propose. Hsieh replied no, insisted his side had made many concessions to UNC views, it was UNC’s turn to make a move.</p>
                <p>“Recessed 1145. Continue tomorrow 1100. Signed Joy”.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
